WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.000
Mentor: So today let's talk about chunking strategy, since it trips up
almost every intern who gets to the embedding pipeline step.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:28.000
Mentor: The core problem is that embedding models have a limited context
window, and even within that window, embedding quality degrades on very
long chunks because the vector ends up averaging too many unrelated
ideas into one point in space.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:45.000
Mentor: So you split documents into chunks small enough to stay
semantically coherent, but you don't want them too small either, or you
lose surrounding context a query might need to actually answer a
question.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:01:05.000
Mentor: EKIE uses roughly 800-word chunks with 120-word overlap. The
overlap matters because if a sentence explaining something important
gets split exactly at a chunk boundary, overlap means it still shows up
in full inside at least one of the two chunks.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:22.000
Intern: Why 800 words specifically, and not something like 200 or 2000?

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Mentor: It's a tradeoff. Too small and you fragment ideas across many
chunks, so semantic search has to guess which fragment matters, and you
lose surrounding context. Too large and a single chunk covers multiple
unrelated topics, diluting the embedding, and it also means RAG stuffs
more irrelevant text into the LLM's context window per retrieved chunk.
800 words with 120 overlap is a reasonable middle ground for
prose-heavy documentation, though you'd tune it differently for, say,
code files.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:02:10.000
Mentor: One more thing — chunking runs on the embedding-facing text
only. The knowledge graph's entity extraction step deliberately does NOT
reuse these chunks; it runs on the full document text in one pass,
because chunk boundaries would fragment sentences and undercount or
duplicate entity co-occurrence relationships.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:20.000
Intern: Got it — so chunking is tuned for retrieval, not for NER.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:25.000
Mentor: Exactly.
